{
  "id": "minimum-depth-of-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "minimum-depth-of-binary-tree",
  "slug": "minimum-depth-of-binary-tree",
  "title": "Minimum Depth of Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this looks like maximum depth with min instead of max - but what goes wrong if you naively take 1 + min(depth(left), depth(right)) when one child is missing?",
    "a missing child would contribute a depth of 0, making 1 + min(...) equal to 1 - incorrectly suggesting a leaf exists right there, even though the real path must continue down the other side",
    "handle three cases explicitly: both children missing (it's a leaf, return 1), exactly one child missing (recurse into the side that exists, add 1), both children present (1 + min of the two recursive depths)",
    "define depth(missing node) as returning a special 'not applicable' signal internally, or just structure the recursion so a missing child is never combined with min() against a present sibling"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "reusing the maximum-depth logic verbatim with min() swapped in for max(), which breaks on nodes with exactly one child",
    "treating the root itself as missing (index out of bounds or -101 value) but still returning 1 instead of 0",
    "computing the answer as the minimum leaf depth found anywhere via a separate traversal, without connecting it correctly to root-to-leaf paths specifically"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap)\nindexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- either index only counts as a real child if it's within bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" and never appears as a real node value\n\n**Return the minimum depth of the tree: the number of nodes along the shortest path from the root down to the nearest leaf (a node with no children).**\n\nA node that has only one child does NOT count as a leaf, you must keep\ndescending on its existing side. An empty tree has depth 0.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 5000",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree ([-101])",
      "single node tree, which has minimum depth 1",
      "a node with exactly one child, where the missing side must not be treated as a depth-0 shortcut"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, -101, -101, 4]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "root 1's left child, 2, has no children of its own, so it's a leaf at depth 2. even though the right branch (1 -> 3 -> 4) goes deeper, the shortest path wins"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, -101, 2, -101, -101, -101, 3]",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "root 1 has no left child, only a right child 2; 2 in turn has no left child, only a right child 3. since 1 and 2 each have exactly one child, neither counts as a leaf - the only leaf is 3, at depth 3"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-101]",
        "output": "0",
        "expectedOutput": "0",
        "explanation": "an empty tree has no nodes, so its minimum depth is 0"
      }
    ],
